==Swedish==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|sv|gmq-osw|ænkti}}, {{m|gmq-osw|ænti}}, {{m|gmq-osw|ingti}}, from a late form of the nominative neuter of {{inh|sv|gmq-osw|ængin}}. For an earlier form, see {{m|sv|icke}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|sv|/²ɪntɛ/}}
** {{audio|sv|Sv-inte.ogg}}
* {{q|[[w:Scanian dialect |Scania]]}} {{IPA|sv|/²ɛntɛ/}}

===Adverb===
{{sv-adv|-}}

# [[not]]
#: {{ux|sv|Myror är '''inte''' stora|Ants are '''not''' big}}
#: {{ux|sv|Hästar är '''inte''' växter|Horses are '''not''' plants}}
#: {{ux|sv|– Är '''inte''' hästar däggdjur? – {{l|sv|jo|Jo}}, hästar är däggdjur.|– Aren't horses mammals? – Yes, horses are mammals.|lit=– Are '''not''' horses mammals? – Yes, horses are mammals.}}
#: {{ux|sv|Jag gillade '''inte''' filmen|I didn't like the movie|lit=I liked '''not''' the movie}}
#: {{ux|sv|Gillade du '''inte''' filmen?|Didn't you like the movie?|lit=Liked you '''not''' the movie?}}
#: {{ux|sv|Jag såg dig '''inte'''|I didn't see you|lit=I saw you '''not'''}}
#: {{ux|sv|Såg du mig '''inte'''?|Didn't you see me?|lit=Saw you me '''not'''?}}
#: {{ux|sv|Jag såg '''inte''' dig, men jag såg Nisse|I didn't see ''you'' [emphasis], but I saw Nisse|lit=I saw '''not''' you, but I saw Nisse}}
#: {{ux|sv|Det är '''inte''' fredag idag|It's '''not''' Friday today}}
#: {{ux|sv|Imorgon är det '''inte''' fredag, utan lördag|Tomorrow is '''not''' Friday, but Saturday|lit=Tomorrow is it '''not''' Friday, but Saturday [''Är'' moves before ''det'' due to V2 word order – no special behavior related to ''inte'' here]}}
#: {{ux|sv|Det regnar '''inte'''|It's not raining|lit=It rains '''not'''}}
#: {{ux|sv|Jag hoppas att det '''inte''' regnar imorgon|I hope it's not raining tomorrow|lit=I hope that it '''not''' rains tomorrow [''Inte'' comes before the main verb in subclauses ("att det inte regnar imorgon" here)}}
#: {{ux|sv|Om det '''inte''' regnar {{l|sv|så|id=adverbial filler}} kan [or {{m|sv|skola|skulle vi kunna}} – ''could'' in tone] vi gå på picknick|If it doesn't rain, we can go on a picnic|lit=If it '''not''' rains [{{m|sv|så|id=adverbial filler}} – optionally appears after an initial adverbial] can we go on picnic ["Om det inte regnar" is a subclause, so ''inte'' comes before ''regnar'', like above)]}}
#: {{ux|sv|Han kan '''inte''' simma|He can't swim|lit=He can '''not''' swim}}
#: {{ux|sv|en man som '''inte''' kan simma|a man who can't swim|lit=a man that '''not''' can swim ["Som inte kan simma" is a subclause, so ''inte'' comes before ''kan'', like above]}}

====Usage notes====
Getting the word order wrong with adverbials and subclauses is extremely common among Swedish learners. It rarely causes any trouble with understanding in practice, just sounding a bit off, like "a man who not can swim" does in English (though it is the correct word order in Swedish).

